.TH CPMEMU 1 "2026-11-24" "cpmemu" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cpmemu \- run CP/M 2.2 programs on a modern host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cpmemu
.RI [ options ]
.I program.com
.RI [ args ...]
.br
.B cpmemu
.RI [ options ]
.I config.cfg
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cpmemu
runs CP/M 2.2 programs.
It emulates a Z80 or an 8080 CPU, and it answers the BDOS and BIOS calls of
the program on the host.
Each CP/M drive is a host directory, and each CP/M file is a host file.
cpmemu needs no CP/M system image and no ROM.
.PP
The first argument that is not an option is the program to run, or a config
file.
An argument that contains
.B .cfg
is a config file.
A config file gives the program, the host directory of each drive, the file
mappings and the devices.
.PP
The arguments after the program go to the program, in upper case, in its
command tail at 0080h and in the two FCBs at 005Ch and 006Ch.
An option written after the program is also an option, and the program does
not get it.
An argument that is not one of the options below goes to the program, so a
tail such as
.B TEST,TEST.COM/N/E
reaches the program whole.
.PP
Console input and output are the terminal of cpmemu.
When stdin is a file or a pipe and the input ends, a BDOS read gets CR once
and then ^Z.
After 1024 reads past the end of input, cpmemu stops the program.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-z80
Emulate a Z80.
This is the default.
.TP
.B \-\-8080
Emulate an 8080.
.TP
.BR \-\-progress [ =\fIN\fR ]
Report progress on stderr every
.I N
million instructions.
Without
.IR N ,
every 100 million.
Off by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-save\-memory= FILE
At the end of the run, write the 64K of memory to
.IR FILE ,
for MOVCPM and SYSGEN.
.TP
.BI \-\-save\-range= S\-E
With
.BR \-\-save\-memory ,
write only the addresses from
.I S
to
.I E
(hexadecimal, for example DC00\-FFFF).
.TP
.BI \-\-int\-cycles= N
Make a timer interrupt every
.I N
CPU cycles.
The CPU is in interrupt mode 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-int\-rst= N
The RST number of the timer interrupt, 0 to 7.
The default is 7 (RST 38H).
.TP
.B \-\-ctrl\-c\-exit
Five ^C typed one after the other end cpmemu.
This is the default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ctrl\-c\-exit
Give every ^C to the program.
Use this for programs that use ^C as a key, such as WordStar.
.TP
.B \-\-eof\-idle
When redirected input has ended, a console status call (BDOS 11, BDOS 6 with
E = FEh or FFh, BIOS CONST) answers "no key", unless the program waits for a
key.
A program that looks for ^C between pieces of work then runs to its end.
This is the default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-eof\-idle
When redirected input has ended, every console status call answers "a key is
ready".
.TP
.BR \-\-gdb [ =\fIPORT\fR ]
Wait for GDB to connect on 127.0.0.1, port
.I PORT
(default 1234; 0 lets the system choose), and stop before the first
instruction at 0100h.
.TP
.BI \-\-gdb\-port\-file= FILE
Write the port that
.B \-\-gdb
listens on to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.BI \-\-gdb\-listen= ADDR
With
.BR \-\-gdb ,
listen on the IPv4 address
.I ADDR
in place of 127.0.0.1.
0.0.0.0 is every interface.
There is no password: a person who can reach
.I ADDR
controls the program.
.TP
.B \-\-backtrace
Keep a shadow call stack, and print the stack on stderr if the run ends
badly.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the option list on stdout and exit.
The program does not run.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version on stdout and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CPM_PROGRESS
Report progress every
.I N
million instructions.
A
.B \-\-progress
option outranks this variable.
.TP
.B CPM_DEBUG
Set to 1, true or yes to turn on debug output.
.TP
.B CPM_PRINTER
The file for LIST output and for the ^P console echo.
.TP
.B CPM_AUX_IN
The file for Reader input.
At the end of the file, or with no file, a read gets ^Z.
.TP
.B CPM_AUX_OUT
The file for Punch output.
.TP
.B CPM_BIOS_DISK
What the BIOS disk calls do:
.B ok
(the default) returns A = 0,
.B fail
returns A = 1, and
.B error
ends cpmemu.
.TP
.B CPM_DEBUG_BDOS
Trace these BDOS functions: decimal numbers, separated by commas.
.TP
.B CPM_DEBUG_BIOS
Trace these BIOS entries: decimal jump\-table offsets, separated by commas.
.PP
cpmemu reads the environment after the config file.
Thus CPM_PRINTER, CPM_AUX_IN and CPM_AUX_OUT replace the matching config
lines.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The program ended: a jump to 0000h, BDOS function 0 or a warm boot.
Also when cpmemu stops the program: after 1024 reads past the end of input,
after five ^C, or at the instruction limit.
.TP
.B 1
cpmemu could not start the program: the program or config file does not
open, there is no program, or the value of an option is not correct (for
example a
.B \-\-gdb
port above 65535).
Also when cpmemu stops the program on an error: a write to a read\-only
drive, a HLT that no interrupt can end, a BIOS disk call with
CPM_BIOS_DISK=error, or a kill from GDB.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a program as a Z80:
.PP
.RS
.B cpmemu program.com
.RE
.PP
Run a program as an 8080:
.PP
.RS
.B cpmemu \-\-8080 program.com
.RE
.PP
Give the program a file name; the program gets FILE.DAT in its command tail
and in its first FCB:
.PP
.RS
.B cpmemu program.com file.dat
.RE
.PP
Run the program, drives and mappings that a config file gives:
.PP
.RS
.B cpmemu config.cfg
.RE
.SH FILES
A config file is a list of
.I key
=
.I value
lines.
The keys include
.BR program ,
.BR cd ,
.B drive_A
to
.BR drive_P ,
.BR default_mode ,
.BR eol_convert ,
.BR ctrl_c_exit ,
.BR eof_idle ,
.BR printer ,
.B aux_input
and
.BR aux_output .
Any other key is a file mapping, for example
.BR "TEST.BAS = /home/user/test.bas text" .
The full reference is config_file.md in the cpmemu documentation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cpm_disk (1)
.PP
